    {
      "id": "3281af67-61e8-4bf0-9afe-e7f4bc677218",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Compare and check – methods\r\n\r\nMethods that **ask a question** about the text and return a `boolean` (or an `int` for `compareTo`).\r\n\r\n- `equals` / `equalsIgnoreCase` → is it the same text?\r\n- `compareTo` / `compareToIgnoreCase` → which one comes first alphabetically?\r\n- `isEmpty` / `isBlank` → is there any text at all?\r\n- `matches(regex)` → does the whole text follow a pattern?\r\n- `contains`, `startsWith`, `endsWith` → already covered in your cells above.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "3281af67-61e8-4bf0-9afe-e7f4bc677218"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "a1c0402a-bc1e-4211-ade3-f1d2cb4e2ad1",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String a = \"John Doe\";\r\nString b = \"John Doe\";\r\nString c = new String(\"John Doe\"); // a new object with the same text\r\n\r\n//this is to compare the text of two strings\r\n// Method: equals(other)\r\n// What:   returns true when both strings have exactly the same characters (case matters)\r\n// Use:    to check if two strings are the same text; never use == for that\r\nSystem.out.println(\"a.equals(b): \" + a.equals(b));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"a.equals(c): \" + a.equals(c));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"a == b:      \" + (a == b));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"a == c:      \" + (a == c)); // false: different objects, same text",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "a1c0402a-bc1e-4211-ade3-f1d2cb4e2ad1",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 1,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "a.equals(b): true\r\na.equals(c): true\r\na == b:      true\r\na == c:      false\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 1
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3a413fda-17de-446b-9408-d284d1c72012",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String name = \"John Doe\";\r\n\r\n//this is to compare text while ignoring upper/lower case\r\n// Method: equalsIgnoreCase(other)\r\n// What:   returns true when both strings have the same characters, treating 'a' and 'A' as equal\r\n// Use:    for user input such as \"yes\" / \"YES\" / \"Yes\", or e-mail addresses\r\nSystem.out.println(\"equals:           \" + name.equals(\"john doe\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"equalsIgnoreCase: \" + name.equalsIgnoreCase(\"john doe\"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "3a413fda-17de-446b-9408-d284d1c72012",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 2,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "equals:           false\r\nequalsIgnoreCase: true\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 2
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "38853c8f-c87f-4440-959d-67029b96dd56",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String a = \"apple\";\r\nString b = \"Banana\";\r\n\r\n//this is to sort text alphabetically\r\n// Method: compareTo(other) / compareToIgnoreCase(other)\r\n// What:   0 when equal, negative when this string comes first, positive when it comes after\r\n// Use:    to sort strings; compareTo puts every capital letter before every small letter, compareToIgnoreCase does not\r\nSystem.out.println(\"apple.compareTo(Banana):           \" + a.compareTo(b));           // positive: 'a' (97) comes after 'B' (66)\r\nSystem.out.println(\"apple.compareToIgnoreCase(Banana): \" + a.compareToIgnoreCase(b)); // negative: a comes before b\r\nSystem.out.println(\"apple.compareTo(apple):            \" + a.compareTo(\"apple\"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "38853c8f-c87f-4440-959d-67029b96dd56",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 3,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "apple.compareTo(Banana):           31\r\napple.compareToIgnoreCase(Banana): -1\r\napple.compareTo(apple):            0\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 3
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "99e1ae68-828b-4cba-9df7-026bdfe3c294",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String empty = \"\";\r\nString spaces = \"     \";\r\nString text = \"John\";\r\n\r\n//this is to check if a string has no text\r\n// Method: isEmpty()\r\n// What:   true only when length() == 0\r\n// Use:    to check that a value was not left empty\r\nSystem.out.println(\"\\\"\\\".isEmpty():      \" + empty.isEmpty());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"\\\"     \\\".isEmpty(): \" + spaces.isEmpty());\r\n\r\n//this is to check if a string is empty or only white space\r\n// Method: isBlank()  [Java 11+]\r\n// What:   true when the string is empty or contains only spaces, tabs or new lines\r\n// Use:    to validate user input: \"   \" is not a real name\r\nSystem.out.println(\"\\\"\\\".isBlank():      \" + empty.isBlank());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"\\\"     \\\".isBlank(): \" + spaces.isBlank());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"\\\"John\\\".isBlank():  \" + text.isBlank());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "99e1ae68-828b-4cba-9df7-026bdfe3c294",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 4,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "\"\".isEmpty():      true\r\n\"     \".isEmpty(): false\r\n\"\".isBlank():      true\r\n\"     \".isBlank(): true\r\n\"John\".isBlank():  false\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 4
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "e478d01d-dd91-4555-8b9a-f0a22e4023e0",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String pin = \"411001\";\r\nString phone = \"98765-43210\";\r\n\r\n//this is to check if the whole text follows a pattern\r\n// Method: matches(regex)\r\n// What:   true when the complete string matches the regular expression (\\\\d = a digit, {6} = exactly six times)\r\n// Use:    to validate formats such as PIN codes, phone numbers or e-mail addresses\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pin is 6 digits:   \" + pin.matches(\"\\\\d{6}\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"phone is 6 digits: \" + phone.matches(\"\\\\d{6}\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"phone is 5-5:      \" + phone.matches(\"\\\\d{5}-\\\\d{5}\"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "e478d01d-dd91-4555-8b9a-f0a22e4023e0",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 5,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "pin is 6 digits:   true\r\nphone is 6 digits: false\r\nphone is 5-5:      true\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 5
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f5d681f1-9d58-477c-8553-3a4b3fb5d2fd",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Search – methods\r\n\r\nMethods that **find a position** in the text. They return an `int` index, or `-1` when nothing is found.\r\n\r\n- `indexOf(text)` → first position.\r\n- `indexOf(text, from)` → first position **at or after** `from`.\r\n- `lastIndexOf(text)` → last position.\r\n- `charAt(index)` → the character at a position (already covered above). Combined with a loop it lets you look at every character.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "f5d681f1-9d58-477c-8553-3a4b3fb5d2fd"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "14eb95c1-77f0-4649-ac10-43fcbdd1202b",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String name = \"My name is John Wick and i worked in a movie called John Wick 4\";\r\n\r\n//this is to find where a text first appears\r\n// Method: indexOf(text) / indexOf(char)\r\n// What:   returns the index of the first match, or -1 when the text is not there\r\n// Use:    to find a position before cutting with substring, or as a faster \"contains\" that also tells you where\r\nSystem.out.println(\"indexOf(\\\"John\\\"):  \" + name.indexOf(\"John\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"indexOf('W'):     \" + name.indexOf('W'));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"indexOf(\\\"Neo\\\"):   \" + name.indexOf(\"Neo\")); // -1 = not found",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "14eb95c1-77f0-4649-ac10-43fcbdd1202b",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 6,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "indexOf(\"John\"):  11\r\nindexOf('W'):     16\r\nindexOf(\"Neo\"):   -1\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 6
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bdd0a46c-bee7-469b-85cc-d6d04a5d0c08",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String name = \"My name is John Wick and i worked in a movie called John Wick 4\";\r\n\r\n//this is to find the next match after a position\r\n// Method: indexOf(text, fromIndex)\r\n// What:   searches only from fromIndex onwards; returns the index or -1\r\n// Use:    to find the second, third… occurrence\r\nint first = name.indexOf(\"John\");\r\nint second = name.indexOf(\"John\", first + 1);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"first John:  \" + first);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"second John: \" + second);\r\n\r\n//this is to find the last match\r\n// Method: lastIndexOf(text)\r\n// What:   searches from the end; returns the index of the last match or -1\r\n// Use:    to find a file extension (\".txt\") or the last word\r\nSystem.out.println(\"lastIndexOf(\\\"John\\\"): \" + name.lastIndexOf(\"John\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"lastIndexOf(\\\" \\\"):    \" + name.lastIndexOf(\" \"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "bdd0a46c-bee7-469b-85cc-d6d04a5d0c08",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 7,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "first John:  11\r\nsecond John: 52\r\nlastIndexOf(\"John\"): 52\r\nlastIndexOf(\" \"):    61\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 7
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "c39e9b75-462e-48b6-b2da-807b466937b0",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String word = \"Mississippi\";\r\nchar letter = 's';\r\nint count = 0;\r\n\r\n//this is to look at every character one by one\r\n// Method: charAt(index) inside a for loop\r\n// What:   the loop index runs from 0 to length() - 1 and charAt gives the character at that position\r\n// Use:    to count, check or change single characters\r\nfor (int i = 0; i < word.length(); i++) {\r\n    if (word.charAt(i) == letter) {\r\n        count++;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\nSystem.out.println(\"'\" + letter + \"' appears \" + count + \" times in \" + word);\r\n\r\n//this is to turn a string into an array of characters\r\n// Method: toCharArray()\r\n// What:   returns a char[] with one slot per character\r\n// Use:    to use a for-each loop over the characters, or to sort / change them\r\nint vowels = 0;\r\nfor (char ch : word.toCharArray()) {\r\n    if (\"aeiouAEIOU\".indexOf(ch) != -1) {\r\n        vowels++;\r\n    }\r\n}\r\nSystem.out.println(\"vowels in \" + word + \": \" + vowels);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "c39e9b75-462e-48b6-b2da-807b466937b0",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 8,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "'s' appears 4 times in Mississippi\r\nvowels in Mississippi: 4\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 8
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "62df4f74-4f52-426e-8676-f05cfc8c19ed",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Change text – methods\r\n\r\nMethods that **return a new string** built from the old one. The original string is never modified (immutability).\r\n\r\n- Cut: `substring(begin)`, `substring(begin, end)`.\r\n- Replace: `replace`, `replaceAll`, `replaceFirst`.\r\n- White space: `trim`, `strip`, `stripLeading`, `stripTrailing`.\r\n- Split and join: `split`, `String.join`.\r\n- Repeat: `repeat`.\r\n- Case: `toUpperCase`, `toLowerCase` (already covered above).",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "62df4f74-4f52-426e-8676-f05cfc8c19ed"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "12e8cb7d-f242-4f3a-b7d4-35dc4660f455",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String name = \"john wick\";\r\n\r\n//this is to show that a String never changes\r\n// Method: (any String method, here toUpperCase())\r\n// What:   the method builds and returns a NEW string; the variable still points to the old one\r\n// Use:    always assign the result back, or to a new variable\r\nname.toUpperCase();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"after name.toUpperCase():        \" + name); // unchanged\r\n\r\nString upper = name.toUpperCase();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"after upper = name.toUpperCase(): \" + upper);\r\n\r\nname = name.toUpperCase();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"after name = name.toUpperCase():  \" + name); // now the variable holds the new string",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "12e8cb7d-f242-4f3a-b7d4-35dc4660f455",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 9,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "after name.toUpperCase():        john wick\r\nafter upper = name.toUpperCase(): JOHN WICK\r\nafter name = name.toUpperCase():  JOHN WICK\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 9
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "90e0aa44-8723-45e0-9bcd-1f702cd6a3ce",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String fileName = \"holiday-photo.jpeg\";\r\n\r\n//this is to cut from a position to the end\r\n// Method: substring(beginIndex)\r\n// What:   returns the characters from beginIndex to the end (beginIndex is included)\r\n// Use:    to get the part after a separator, for example a file extension\r\nint dot = fileName.indexOf(\".\");\r\nString extension = fileName.substring(dot + 1);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"extension: \" + extension);\r\n\r\n//this is to cut a part out of the middle\r\n// Method: substring(beginIndex, endIndex)\r\n// What:   returns the characters from beginIndex up to, but NOT including, endIndex\r\n// Use:    to get the part before a separator, or any slice of the text\r\nString baseName = fileName.substring(0, dot);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"base name: \" + baseName);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"first word: \" + fileName.substring(0, fileName.indexOf(\"-\")));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "90e0aa44-8723-45e0-9bcd-1f702cd6a3ce",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 10,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 10,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "extension: jpeg\r\nbase name: holiday-photo\r\nfirst word: holiday\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 10
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "10e3c83d-337c-4a3d-ab44-ad2685e079d5",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String sentence = \"My name is John Wick and i worked in a movie called John Wick 4\";\r\n\r\n//this is to swap one character for another everywhere\r\n// Method: replace(oldChar, newChar)\r\n// What:   returns a new string with every oldChar changed to newChar\r\n// Use:    to change separators, e.g. spaces to underscores\r\nSystem.out.println(sentence.replace(' ', '_'));\r\n\r\n//this is to swap one piece of text for another everywhere\r\n// Method: replace(oldText, newText)\r\n// What:   returns a new string with EVERY occurrence of oldText changed to newText (plain text, no regex)\r\n// Use:    to fix a word, or to remove text by replacing it with \"\"\r\nSystem.out.println(sentence.replace(\"John Wick\", \"Neo\"));\r\nSystem.out.println(sentence.replace(\" and i worked in a movie called John Wick 4\", \"\"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "10e3c83d-337c-4a3d-ab44-ad2685e079d5",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 11,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 11,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "My_name_is_John_Wick_and_i_worked_in_a_movie_called_John_Wick_4\r\nMy name is Neo and i worked in a movie called Neo 4\r\nMy name is John Wick\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 11
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "21925ff7-4a55-4cc9-8bb8-330c7d4aef8c",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String messy = \"Order   number:  1234,   total:  99\";\r\n\r\n//this is to replace using a pattern (regular expression)\r\n// Method: replaceAll(regex, replacement)\r\n// What:   every match of the pattern is replaced; \\\\s+ = one or more white-space characters, \\\\d = a digit\r\n// Use:    to clean up repeated spaces, or to hide/replace all digits at once\r\nSystem.out.println(messy.replaceAll(\"\\\\s+\", \" \"));\r\nSystem.out.println(messy.replaceAll(\"\\\\d\", \"#\"));\r\n\r\n//this is to replace only the first match of a pattern\r\n// Method: replaceFirst(regex, replacement)\r\n// What:   like replaceAll but stops after the first match\r\n// Use:    when only the first occurrence should change\r\nSystem.out.println(messy.replaceFirst(\"\\\\d+\", \"####\"));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "21925ff7-4a55-4cc9-8bb8-330c7d4aef8c",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 12,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 12,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Order number: 1234, total: 99\r\nOrder   number:  ####,   total:  ##\r\nOrder   number:  ####,   total:  99\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 12
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "a60e0e2f-0852-400a-b87d-86cd8097276a",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String padded = \"   John Wick   \";\r\n\r\n//this is to remove spaces at both ends\r\n// Method: trim() / strip()  [strip: Java 11+]\r\n// What:   both return the text without leading and trailing white space; strip also understands Unicode spaces\r\n// Use:    to clean user input before saving or comparing it\r\nSystem.out.println(\"[\" + padded.trim() + \"]\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"[\" + padded.strip() + \"]\");\r\n\r\n//this is to remove spaces at only one end\r\n// Method: stripLeading() / stripTrailing()  [Java 11+]\r\n// What:   removes white space only at the start / only at the end\r\n// Use:    when the other side's spacing must stay as it is\r\nSystem.out.println(\"[\" + padded.stripLeading() + \"]\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"[\" + padded.stripTrailing() + \"]\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"lengths: \" + padded.length() + \" -> \" + padded.strip().length());",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "a60e0e2f-0852-400a-b87d-86cd8097276a",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 13,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 13,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "[John Wick]\r\n[John Wick]\r\n[John Wick   ]\r\n[   John Wick]\r\nlengths: 15 -> 9\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 13
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5aad2dcb-4fb0-4890-8ad4-eaf8dee9961d",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String csv = \"Zoya,Jane,,Doe\";\r\nString spaced = \"one   two three\";\r\n\r\n//this is to cut a string into pieces at a separator\r\n// Method: split(regex)\r\n// What:   returns a String[]; the separator is a regular expression, so \".\" or \"|\" must be escaped as \"\\\\.\" / \"\\\\|\"\r\n// Use:    to read comma-separated values or words of a sentence\r\nString[] names = csv.split(\",\");\r\nSystem.out.println(\"pieces: \" + names.length + \" -> \" + Arrays.toString(names)); // the empty piece between ,, is kept\r\n\r\n//this is to split on any amount of white space\r\n// Method: split(\"\\\\s+\")\r\n// What:   \\\\s+ matches one or more spaces/tabs, so repeated spaces do not create empty pieces\r\n// Use:    to get the words of text with irregular spacing\r\nSystem.out.println(\"words:  \" + Arrays.toString(spaced.split(\"\\\\s+\")));\r\n\r\n//this is to limit the number of pieces\r\n// Method: split(regex, limit)\r\n// What:   splits at most limit - 1 times; the last piece keeps the rest of the text\r\n// Use:    to separate a key from a value when the value may itself contain the separator\r\nSystem.out.println(\"limit 2: \" + Arrays.toString(\"key=value=with=equals\".split(\"=\", 2)));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "5aad2dcb-4fb0-4890-8ad4-eaf8dee9961d",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 14,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 14,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "pieces: 4 -> [Zoya, Jane, , Doe]\r\nwords:  [one, two, three]\r\nlimit 2: [key, value=with=equals]\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 14
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b8458b52-8bc7-4c9a-b1d6-d6d357563c76",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//this is to repeat text\r\n// Method: repeat(count)  [Java 11+]\r\n// What:   returns the string glued to itself count times\r\n// Use:    for separators, padding or simple patterns\r\nSystem.out.println(\"-\".repeat(20));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"ab\".repeat(3));\r\n\r\n//this is to glue many pieces together with a separator\r\n// Method: String.join(separator, pieces...) / String.join(separator, list)\r\n// What:   static method: puts the separator between every piece, with none at the ends\r\n// Use:    the opposite of split: to build \"a, b, c\" from a list\r\nSystem.out.println(String.join(\", \", \"Zoya\", \"Jane\", \"Doe\"));\r\n\r\nList<String> cities = List.of(\"Mumbai\", \"Delhi\", \"Pune\");\r\nSystem.out.println(String.join(\" -> \", cities));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"-\".repeat(20));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "b8458b52-8bc7-4c9a-b1d6-d6d357563c76",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 15,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 15,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "--------------------\r\nababab\r\nZoya, Jane, Doe\r\nMumbai -> Delhi -> Pune\r\n--------------------\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 15
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1bb9d983-39b5-4719-8295-0e170cb0f841",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Convert and format – methods\r\n\r\nGoing **between text and other types**, and building text with placeholders.\r\n\r\n- Number → text: `String.valueOf(42)`, `Integer.toString(42)`, or simply `42 + \"\"`.\r\n- Text → number: `Integer.parseInt(\"42\")`, `Double.parseDouble(\"3.5\")`. Fails with `NumberFormatException` if the text is not a number.\r\n- `toCharArray()` → `char[]`, and `new String(char[])` back again.\r\n- `String.format(...)` / `\"...\".formatted(...)` → fill placeholders like `%s` and `%d`.\r\n- Text blocks `\"\"\" ... \"\"\"` (Java 15+) → multi-line strings without `\\n`.\r\n- `Character.isDigit`, `Character.isLetter`, `Character.toUpperCase` → questions about a single `char`.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "1bb9d983-39b5-4719-8295-0e170cb0f841"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "b1610228-252a-4d76-93af-bce6f4e596df",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "int age = 20;\r\ndouble price = 99.5;\r\nboolean active = true;\r\n\r\n//this is to turn a number (or anything) into text\r\n// Method: String.valueOf(x) / Integer.toString(n)\r\n// What:   static methods that return the value as a String\r\n// Use:    before calling String methods on a number (e.g. to count its digits), or to build messages\r\nString ageText = String.valueOf(age);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"digits in age: \" + ageText.length());\r\nSystem.out.println(\"Integer.toString(age): \" + Integer.toString(age));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"String.valueOf(price): \" + String.valueOf(price));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"String.valueOf(active): \" + String.valueOf(active));\r\n\r\n// the shortcut: joining with \"\" also converts\r\nString quick = age + \"\";\r\nSystem.out.println(\"age + \\\"\\\" -> \\\"\" + quick + \"\\\" (length \" + quick.length() + \")\");",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "b1610228-252a-4d76-93af-bce6f4e596df",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 16,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 16,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "digits in age: 2\r\nInteger.toString(age): 20\r\nString.valueOf(price): 99.5\r\nString.valueOf(active): true\r\nage + \"\" -> \"20\" (length 2)\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 16
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cd53238e-c682-43a5-8dad-c9b864b5cca3",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String ageText = \"20\";\r\nString priceText = \"99.5\";\r\n\r\n//this is to turn text into a number\r\n// Method: Integer.parseInt(text) / Double.parseDouble(text)\r\n// What:   static methods that read the text and return an int / double; throw NumberFormatException if the text is not a number\r\n// Use:    user input and file contents always arrive as text; convert before doing maths\r\nint age = Integer.parseInt(ageText);\r\ndouble price = Double.parseDouble(priceText);\r\n\r\nSystem.out.println(\"age + 1 as text:   \" + (ageText + 1)); // \"201\": text joining, not maths\r\nSystem.out.println(\"age + 1 as number: \" + (age + 1));     // 21\r\nSystem.out.println(\"price * 2:         \" + (price * 2));\r\n\r\n// Integer.parseInt(\"abc\");   // ❌ NumberFormatException: For input string: \"abc\"\r\n// Integer.parseInt(\" 20 \");  // ❌ spaces are not allowed either; use \" 20 \".trim() first",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "cd53238e-c682-43a5-8dad-c9b864b5cca3",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 17,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 17,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "age + 1 as text:   201\r\nage + 1 as number: 21\r\nprice * 2:         199.0\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 17
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ccc7f57b-f535-4eb8-8f3c-623c1f59e785",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String name = \"John Wick\";\r\nint movies = 4;\r\ndouble rating = 7.45;\r\n\r\n//this is to build text with placeholders\r\n// Method: String.format(template, values...) / template.formatted(values...)  [formatted: Java 15+]\r\n// What:   %s = any value as text, %d = whole number, %.1f = decimal with 1 digit after the point, %n = new line\r\n// Use:    to build neat messages and tables without many + signs\r\nString line = String.format(\"%s acted in %d movies with a rating of %.1f\", name, movies, rating);\r\nSystem.out.println(line);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"%s acted in %d movies\".formatted(name, movies));\r\n\r\n// width: %-10s pads to 10 characters on the right, %5d pads to 5 on the left\r\nSystem.out.println(String.format(\"|%-10s|%5d|\", \"Name\", 1));\r\nSystem.out.println(String.format(\"|%-10s|%5d|\", \"John Wick\", 4));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "ccc7f57b-f535-4eb8-8f3c-623c1f59e785",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 18,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 18,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "John Wick acted in 4 movies with a rating of 7.5\r\nJohn Wick acted in 4 movies\r\n|Name      |    1|\r\n|John Wick |    4|\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 18
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "605a0f65-af58-4ba1-b014-afc7aa616ee7",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//this is to write a multi-line string\r\n// Method: text block \"\"\" ... \"\"\"  [Java 15+]\r\n// What:   everything between the opening \"\"\" (on its own line) and the closing \"\"\" is the string, line breaks included\r\n// Use:    for HTML, JSON, SQL or any text that spans several lines, instead of many \"...\" + \"\\\\n\"\r\nString json = \"\"\"\r\n    {\r\n      \"name\": \"John Wick\",\r\n      \"movies\": 4\r\n    }\r\n    \"\"\";\r\nSystem.out.println(json);\r\n\r\n// the old way, for comparison\r\nString oldWay = \"{\\n\" +\r\n                \"  \\\"name\\\": \\\"John Wick\\\",\\n\" +\r\n                \"  \\\"movies\\\": 4\\n\" +\r\n                \"}\";\r\nSystem.out.println(oldWay.equals(json.strip()));",
      "metadata": {
