    {
      "id": "43a72aba-539a-4897-98a2-cdeae2cffd29",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "String password = \"Wick2024\";\r\nint letters = 0;\r\nint digits = 0;\r\nint capitals = 0;\r\n\r\n//this is to ask questions about a single character\r\n// Method: Character.isLetter(ch) / Character.isDigit(ch) / Character.isUpperCase(ch)\r\n// What:   static helper methods on the Character class that return true / false for one char\r\n// Use:    to validate passwords, count letters vs digits, check input one character at a time\r\nfor (char ch : password.toCharArray()) {\r\n    if (Character.isLetter(ch)) letters++;\r\n    if (Character.isDigit(ch)) digits++;\r\n    if (Character.isUpperCase(ch)) capitals++;\r\n}\r\nSystem.out.println(\"letters: \" + letters + \", digits: \" + digits + \", capitals: \" + capitals);\r\n\r\n//this is to change the case of one character\r\n// Method: Character.toUpperCase(ch) / Character.toLowerCase(ch)\r\n// What:   returns the changed char (the String versions change the whole string)\r\n// Use:    to capitalise only the first letter of a word\r\nString word = \"mumbai\";\r\nString capitalised = Character.toUpperCase(word.charAt(0)) + word.substring(1);\r\nSystem.out.println(capitalised);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "43a72aba-539a-4897-98a2-cdeae2cffd29",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 20,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 20,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "letters: 4, digits: 4, capitals: 1\r\nMumbai\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 20
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "bca8e46a-96f3-4247-aadd-95b241e790d2",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# StringBuilder – properties and methods\r\n\r\n`StringBuilder` (from `java.lang`) is a **mutable** piece of text: unlike `String`, its methods change the object itself.\r\n\r\n**Properties**\r\n- **Mutable**: `append`, `insert`, `delete`, `reverse`… change the builder in place and also return it, so calls can be chained: `sb.append(\"a\").append(\"b\")`.\r\n- Much **faster** than `+=` inside a loop. `s += \"x\"` creates a brand-new `String` every time; `sb.append(\"x\")` just adds to the same buffer.\r\n- Has a **capacity** (default 16 characters) that grows automatically, like an `ArrayList`.\r\n- Call `toString()` to get a normal `String` at the end.\r\n- **Not synchronized** (not thread-safe). Its thread-safe twin is `StringBuffer` (same methods, slower).\r\n- No `equals` by content: `sb1.equals(sb2)` compares references. Compare with `sb1.toString().equals(sb2.toString())` or `sb1.compareTo(sb2)` (Java 11+).",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "bca8e46a-96f3-4247-aadd-95b241e790d2"
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "6574565f-e6b9-4773-a297-9115a027cad7",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "//Syntax\r\n//StringBuilder variable_name = new StringBuilder();\r\n\r\n//this is to create an empty builder\r\n// Method: new StringBuilder() / new StringBuilder(\"start text\")\r\n// What:   creates a mutable text buffer (default capacity 16, grows when needed)\r\n// Use:    when you will add to the text many times\r\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\r\n\r\n//this is to add text to the end\r\n// Method: append(x)\r\n// What:   adds x (any type: String, char, int, boolean…) at the end and returns the same builder, so calls can be chained\r\n// Use:    to build a result step by step\r\nsb.append(\"John\").append(' ').append(\"Wick\").append(' ').append(4);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"builder: \" + sb);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"length:  \" + sb.length());\r\n\r\n//this is to get a normal String back\r\n// Method: toString()\r\n// What:   returns the current content as an immutable String\r\n// Use:    when you are done building and need a String (to store, compare or return it)\r\nString result = sb.toString();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"string:  \" + result);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "6574565f-e6b9-4773-a297-9115a027cad7",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 21,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 21,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "builder: John Wick 4\r\nlength:  11\r\nstring:  John Wick 4\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 21
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b04d1dbd-0591-442c-8f15-487060afd7cd",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"John Wick\");\r\n\r\n//this is to add text at a position\r\n// Method: insert(index, x)\r\n// What:   puts x at index and shifts the rest to the right (changes the builder itself)\r\n// Use:    to add a prefix or a word in the middle\r\nsb.insert(0, \"Mr. \");\r\nSystem.out.println(sb);\r\n\r\n//this is to replace a range of characters\r\n// Method: replace(start, end, text)\r\n// What:   removes the characters from start up to (not including) end and puts text there\r\n// Use:    to change a part of the text by position (String.replace works by content instead)\r\nsb.replace(4, 8, \"Neo\");\r\nSystem.out.println(sb);\r\n\r\n//this is to remove characters\r\n// Method: delete(start, end) / deleteCharAt(index)\r\n// What:   delete removes a range (end excluded); deleteCharAt removes one character\r\n// Use:    to cut text out without building a new string\r\nsb.delete(0, 4);\r\nSystem.out.println(sb);\r\nsb.deleteCharAt(sb.length() - 1);\r\nSystem.out.println(sb);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "b04d1dbd-0591-442c-8f15-487060afd7cd",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 22,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 22,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "Mr. John Wick\r\nMr. Neo Wick\r\nNeo Wick\r\nNeo Wic\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 22
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6f772889-d8d7-48e6-9909-bf2756e626e7",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"level\");\r\n\r\n//this is to flip the text\r\n// Method: reverse()\r\n// What:   reverses the characters in place and returns the builder\r\n// Use:    to check for palindromes or reverse a word (String has no reverse method)\r\nString reversed = sb.reverse().toString();\r\nSystem.out.println(\"reversed: \" + reversed);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"is 'level' a palindrome? \" + \"level\".equals(reversed));\r\n\r\n//this is to change one character\r\n// Method: setCharAt(index, ch) / charAt(index)\r\n// What:   setCharAt overwrites the character at index (a String cannot do this); charAt reads it\r\n// Use:    to fix a single letter without rebuilding the text\r\nStringBuilder name = new StringBuilder(\"jack\");\r\nname.setCharAt(0, Character.toUpperCase(name.charAt(0)));\r\nSystem.out.println(\"fixed:    \" + name);",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "6f772889-d8d7-48e6-9909-bf2756e626e7",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 23,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 23,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "reversed: level\r\nis 'level' a palindrome? true\r\nfixed:    Jack\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 23
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "646d563c-8b9d-4ad1-894f-6a6acf37c26d",
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "List<String> names = List.of(\"Zoya\", \"Jane\", \"Doe\", \"Arbaz\");\r\n\r\n//this is to build text in a loop the slow way\r\n// Method: += on a String\r\n// What:   every += creates a NEW String and copies all the old characters into it\r\n// Use:    fine for 2 or 3 joins; avoid inside loops with many iterations\r\nString slow = \"\";\r\nfor (String n : names) {\r\n    slow += n + \", \";\r\n}\r\nSystem.out.println(\"with +=:            \" + slow);\r\n\r\n//this is to build text in a loop the fast way\r\n// Method: StringBuilder.append in a loop + setLength(n)\r\n// What:   append adds to the same buffer; setLength(n) cuts the text to n characters (here: drops the last \", \")\r\n// Use:    whenever you join many pieces: logs, reports, CSV lines\r\nStringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\r\nfor (String n : names) {\r\n    sb.append(n).append(\", \");\r\n}\r\nsb.setLength(sb.length() - 2);\r\nSystem.out.println(\"with StringBuilder: \" + sb);\r\n\r\n// Same result in one line, when you only need a separator:\r\nSystem.out.println(\"with String.join:   \" + String.join(\", \", names));",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "java",
        "id": "646d563c-8b9d-4ad1-894f-6a6acf37c26d",
        "executionSummary": {
          "executionOrder": 24,
          "success": true
        }
      },
      "execution_count": 24,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "output_type": "execute_result",
          "data": {
            "text/plain": "with +=:            Zoya, Jane, Doe, Arbaz, \r\nwith StringBuilder: Zoya, Jane, Doe, Arbaz\r\nwith String.join:   Zoya, Jane, Doe, Arbaz\r\n"
          },
          "metadata": {},
          "execution_count": 24
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b97e14e6-6195-4d21-9afb-97e2583a3425",
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# String vs StringBuilder vs StringBuffer\r\n\r\n| | `String` | `StringBuilder` | `StringBuffer` |\r\n|---|---|---|---|\r\n| Can it change? | ❌ Immutable (methods return a new string) | ✅ Mutable (methods change it in place) | ✅ Mutable |\r\n| Thread-safe | ✅ (because it never changes) | ❌ | ✅ Synchronized |\r\n| Speed when building text in a loop | Slow (`+=` copies every time) | Fastest | Slower than `StringBuilder` (locking) |\r\n| Compare content | `equals(other)` | `toString().equals(...)` or `compareTo` | `toString().equals(...)` or `compareTo` |\r\n| Package | `java.lang` | `java.lang` | `java.lang` |\r\n| Use when | Text that does not change: names, messages, keys | Building or editing text step by step | Same, but shared between threads (rare) |\r\n\r\n**Rule of thumb:** use `String` everywhere by default. Switch to `StringBuilder` only when you build text in a loop or edit it many times.",
      "metadata": {
        "language": "markdown",
        "id": "b97e14e6-6195-4d21-9afb-97e2583a3425"
      }
    }
  ]
}